1: # Actual ordinary products and circle traces on every lattice-basis fan
2: 
3: This working proof retains the selected-cusp positive rho loop of F54/F56,
4: its original physical height q, fixed scalar, both finite-cap profiles, and
5: the full Tate/Cartier units. It constructs a product on each specified fan
6: and computes its local trace maps. The resulting relations concern the
7: actual variation and derivative on that fan. They do not substitute the
8: initial-fan return for a return whose flop comparison is uncomputed.
9: 
10: ## GF.1. The actual character and period changes
11: 
12: Let \(P\) be an integral two-by-two matrix with determinant \(+1\) or \(-1\), and
13: write \(R=P^{-1}\). The fan here is the image under \(P\) of OE.1: its two
14: triangles at each lattice point \(n\) have vertices
15: 
16: \[
17:  n,\ n+Pe_1,\ n+Pe_2,
18:  \qquad n+Pe_1,\ n+Pe_2,\ n+P(e_1+e_2).
19: \]
20: 
21: These are the specified two triangles in each period parallelogram.
22: The homogeneous lattice map is \((x,h)\) to \((Px,h)\); its determinant is
23: \(\det P\), and its inverse is integral. On the dense torus use the literal
24: holomorphic character change
25: 
26: \[
27:  Z'=Z^{R_{11}}V^{R_{12}},\qquad
28:  V'=Z^{R_{21}}V^{R_{22}},\qquad q'=q.                 \tag{GF.1}
29: \]
30: 
31: Its inverse uses the rows of \(P\). Thus it extends to an isomorphism
32: between the toric charts with the two matched fans; on each matched
33: unimodular triangle it is the identity in the corresponding three
34: character coordinates. This is a coordinate description of the actual
35: chosen fan filling. The original source and line coordinates on its
36: overlap with the fixed exterior are still obtained by the inverse of
37: (GF.1), so the source and all finite caps are retained.
38: 
39: The original commuting periods are exactly OE.2:
40: 
41: \[
42:  h:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha V,q),\qquad
43:  B:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(gZ,\eta qV,q),\qquad\eta=\lambda\beta.
44: \]
45: 
46: Replace their basis by \(h'_j=h^{P_{1j}}B^{P_{2j}}\), for \(j=1,2\).
47: This has the inverse integral period change \(R\) and therefore gives
48: the same actual quotient. In the characters (GF.1) the two periods
49: are
50: 
51: \[
52:  h'_1:(Z',V')\longmapsto(qA_{11}Z',A_{21}V'),\qquad
53:  h'_2:(Z',V')\longmapsto(A_{12}Z',qA_{22}V'),           \tag{GF.2}
54: \]
55: 
56: where every complete multiplier is retained:
57: 
58: \[
59:  A_{ij}=(\kappa^{P_{1j}}g^{P_{2j}})^{R_{i1}}
60:                 (\alpha^{P_{1j}}\eta^{P_{2j}})^{R_{i2}}.
61:                                                                \tag{GF.3}
62: \]
63: 
64: The power of \(q\) in the \(i\)-th character of \(h'_j\) is \((RP)_{ij}\).
65: This proves the height factors in (GF.2), including their signs.
66: The units are nonzero on the same protected collars as the original
67: ones. At \(q=0\) their parameter winding matrix is exactly
68: 
69: \[
70:  B_P=(b_{ij})=-6P^{-1}e_1e_1^TP,
71:                     \qquad\operatorname{tr}B_P=-6.   \tag{GF.4}
72: \]
73: 
74: There is no omitted unit homotopy in this identity. Write the based
75: ratio of \(\kappa\) as \(\exp(-6i\theta)\) times \(\exp(f_\kappa)\), and write the
76: based ratios of \(\alpha,g,\eta\) as \(\exp(f_\alpha)\), \(\exp(f_g)\), \(\exp(f_\eta)\).
77: The four \(f\)'s are their actual based periodic logarithms from F56.
78: The periodic logarithm of the ratio \(A_{ij}/A_{ij,*}\) after its displayed
79: angular factor is
80: 
81: \[
82:  R_{i1}(P_{1j}f_\kappa+P_{2j}f_g)
83:        +R_{i2}(P_{1j}f_\alpha+P_{2j}f_\eta).          \tag{GF.5}
84: \]
85: 
86: This formula supplies every subsequent unit homotopy.
87: 
88: ## GF.2. The two vertices and the three full normal loops
89: 
90: In the new character coordinates put \((a,b,c)=T^+_0\) and
91: \((u,v,w)=T^-_0\). Direct substitution in (GF.2) gives
92: 
93: \[
94: \begin{array}{c|ccc}
95:  &u&v&w\\ \hline
96:  C_0&A_{11}A_{21}bc&A_{21}^{-1}ab&A_{11}^{-1}b^{-1}\\
97:  C_1&A_{12}A_{22}bc&A_{22}^{-1}c^{-1}&A_{12}^{-1}ac\\
98:  C_2&a^{-1}&ab&ac.
99: \end{array}                                                   \tag{GF.6}
100: \]
101: 
102: Each triple product is \(q\). Keep the first vertex coordinates and
103: use \((u,A_{22}v,A_{11}w)\) at the second, with the corresponding
104: initial constant units in the reference fibre. The tangent
105: coordinates on each curve are then literal reciprocals. The
106: ordered normal pairs are the same as OP.1, and their normal
107: transition matrices are \(zD_i\) with
108: 
109: \[
110:  D_0=\operatorname{diag}(A_{22}/A_{21},A_{11}A_{21}),\quad
111:  D_1=\operatorname{diag}(A_{11}/A_{12},A_{12}A_{22}),\quad
112:  D_2=\operatorname{diag}(A_{22},A_{11}).                \tag{GF.7}
113: \]
114: 
115: For the based loops \(K_i=D_{i,*}^{-1}D_i\), the first diagonal
116: exponents and the sum of the two exponents are therefore
117: 
118: \[
119:  l_0=b_{22}-b_{21},\qquad l_1=b_{11}-b_{12},\qquad
120:  l_2=b_{22},\qquad l_i+\widetilde l_i=-6.              \tag{GF.8}
121: \]
122: 
123: Every determinant has the same even winding \(-6\), including matrices
124: \(P\) whose entries have arbitrary signs. We now construct the actual
125: normal contractions, rather than infer them from that parity.
126: 
127: For an integer \(d\) define
128: 
129: \[
130:  H_d(s,\theta)=
131:  (\sin\vartheta(s)e^{id\theta}+j\cos\vartheta(s))
132:                     (\sin\vartheta(s)+j\cos\vartheta(s))^{-1},
133:                                                                \tag{GF.9}
134: \]
135: 
136: where \(\vartheta\) is OP.6's fixed smooth radial function. These maps
137: are smooth, based, and periodic, and satisfy \(H_d(0,\theta)=1\),
138: \(H_d(1,\theta)=\exp(id\theta)\). Put
139: 
140: \[
141:  A=H_{-3},\qquad B_i=H_{l_i+3},\qquad
142:                   T_i(Q)=A Q B_i,
143:  \quad Q=n_0+j\overline{n_1}.                         \tag{GF.10}
144: \]
145: 
146: At \(s=1\) its two complex normal factors are \(\exp(il_i\theta)\)
147: and \(\exp(i(-6-l_i)\theta)\), respectively: left and right quaternion
148: phases with exponents \(x,y\) give complex exponents \(x+y,x-y\).
149: Thus (GF.10) is the required contraction of each angular loop.
150: Multiplying by the diagonal exponentials from (GF.5), with OP.9's
151: radial cutoff, gives a contraction of the full \(K_i\). In particular
152: the original units, norms, and reference values remain present.
153: 
154: At \(P=I\) the right factor on \(C_0\) and \(C_2\) is \(H_3\), while OP.8 used
155: \(H_{-3}^{-1}\). We retain their exact comparison. Set \(a=\sin\vartheta\),
156: \(b=\cos\vartheta\) and \(n=ai-bk\), a unit imaginary quaternion. The
157: expansion of (GF.9) is
158: 
159: \[
160:  H_d=b^2+a^2\cos(d\theta)
161:        +ab(1-\cos(d\theta))j+a\sin(d\theta)n.
162: \]
163: 
164: Conjugation by \(n\) fixes \(n\) and sends \(j\) to \(-j\). Consequently
165: \(nH_d^{-1}n^{-1}=H_{-d}\). The explicit path
166: 
167: \[
168:  C_t=\cos(\pi t/2)+n\sin(\pi t/2),\qquad
169:  C_tH_{-3}^{-1}C_t^{-1},\qquad 0\leq t\leq1,
170:                                                                \tag{GF.10a}
171: \]
172: 
173: joins OP's right factor to \(H_3\). It is fixed at \(s=0\), where \(H=1\),
174: and at \(s=1\), where \(n=i\) commutes with the diagonal endpoint. It is
175: also fixed at both parameter endpoints. Thus it glues with the
176: protected vertex collars and the carried unit maps to an actual
177: based homotopy of the two entire plumbing products. This verifies
178: the comparison to the initial matrices of K51/K52.
179: 
180: Send \((z,n)\) to \((z,K_i(s(|z|),\theta)n)\) on each tube. The exact
181: collar identity \(zD_{i,*}K_i=zD_i\) glues this to the two vertex
182: maps. The normal rescaling \(R_t(z,n)=(z,tn)\), followed by the
183: cutoff flow of its velocity, carries the original higher normal
184: terms and their protected vertex collars as in OP.3. The curves'
185: interiors are disjoint, so these three flows have the stated
186: disjoint supports. This constructs a periodic oriented ordinary
187: product of the entire labelled plumbing on this actual fan.
188: 
189: Each separate native star is also explicit. Its second coordinates
190: are, respectively,
191: 
192: \[
193: \begin{array}{c|ccc}
194:  C_0&z'=A_{11}w&n'_0=A_{21}v&n'_1=(A_{11}A_{21})^{-1}u\\
195:  C_1&z'=A_{22}v&n'_0=A_{12}w&n'_1=(A_{12}A_{22})^{-1}u\\
196:  C_2&z'=u&n'_0=v&n'_1=w.
197: \end{array}                                                   \tag{GF.11}
198: \]
199: 
200: The first normal pairs are \((a,c),(a,b),(b,c)\). Each row gives
201: exactly \(z'=z^{-1}\), \(n'=zn\). These are actual periodic holomorphic
202: star products with the complete units shown. The ordinary product
203: comparison on its normal Hopf boundary is
204: 
205: \[
206:               q_H\longmapsto (U(z)AU(z)^{-1})q_HB_i.
207:                                                                \tag{GF.12}
208: \]
209: 
210: The left factor is precisely OP.20 and has degree \(-6\) on the
211: Hopf-oriented base sphere times the parameter. The right factor
212: has only radial and parameter variables and has degree zero.
213: The explicit cylinder extension and orientation of OR.2 give
214: the pure-left endpoint \(L^6\). Thus on every separate star the
215: ordinary relative return is the native relative return composed
216: with \(L^6\); its right integer is unchanged. This is an actual
217: boundary-product comparison on the given fan.
218: 
219: ## GF.3. Physical weights and exact local orbit maps
220: 
221: The physical delta character in the new coordinates has integral
222: weights \((\xi,\upsilon)^T=Re_2\). At the first vertex its weights on
223: \((a,b,c)\) are \((-\xi-\upsilon,\xi,\upsilon)\); at the second they are
224: \((\xi+\upsilon,-\upsilon,-\xi)\). Define the tangent and normal-difference
225: weights in the above ordered pairs by
226: 
227: \[
228: \begin{array}{c|rr}
229:  &w_i&d_i\\ \hline
230:  C_0&\xi&-\xi-2\upsilon\\
231:  C_1&\upsilon&-2\xi-\upsilon\\
232:  C_2&-\xi-\upsilon&\xi-\upsilon.
233: \end{array}                                                   \tag{GF.13}
234: \]
235: 
236: The original physical normal action on \(Q\) is left multiplication
237: by \(\exp(-iw_i\phi/2)\) and right multiplication by \(\exp(id_i\phi/2)\).
238: In the ordinary product (GF.10) it becomes exactly
239: 
240: \[
241:  Q\longmapsto
242:  (A e^{-iw_i\phi/2}A^{-1})Q
243:                  (B_i^{-1}e^{id_i\phi/2}B_i).         \tag{GF.14}
244: \]
245: 
246: The full unit version is obtained by the actual conjugations in
247: (GF.5), with the same based homotopy. Its source point moves by
248: \(z\) to \(\exp(iw_i\phi)z\). On the positive radial path \(z=\tan\chi\),
249: write \(U=\cos\chi+j\sin\chi\) and \(E_i=\exp(iw_i\phi/2)\). The moved
250: Hopf representative is exactly \(E_i^{-1}UE_i\). At \(q_H=1\) the
251: normal-sphere orbit trace is consequently
252: 
253: \[
254:  F_i=E_i^{-1}UE_i A E_i^{-1}A^{-1}U^{-1}
255:                        B_i^{-1}e^{id_i\phi/2}B_i.     \tag{GF.15}
256: \]
257: 
258: This includes \(w_i=0\): its left \(A\) factors cancel and the result is
259: the right-axis map in (GF.14). Every expression closes at \(\phi=2\pi\)
260: because \(w_i\) and \(d_i\) have the same parity. It is periodic in \(\theta\),
261: and its radial-end values are \(\theta\)-independent.
262: 
263: For completeness the axis degree in (GF.9) is computed directly.
264: For \(d=0\) the map is constant. For \(d\) nonzero, the inverse images
265: of \(-i\) under \(H_diH_d^{-1}\) have \(\vartheta=\pi/4\) and \(\exp(id\theta)=-1\).
266: There are \(|d|\) of them. At each, \(H_d=j\), and differentiation gives
267: 
268: \[
269:  \partial_s(H_diH_d^{-1})=4\vartheta'(s)k,\qquad
270:  \partial_\theta(H_diH_d^{-1})=-d j.
271: \]
272: 
273: Since \((k,j)\) is the positive outward tangent basis at \(-i\), the
274: degree is \(-d\). For \(H_d^{-1}iH_d\) the first derivative changes
275: sign and the second remains \(-dj\), giving degree \(d\).
276: Thus the axes \(AiA^{-1}\) and \(B_i^{-1}iB_i\) have degrees
277: \(3\) and \(l_i+3\). Conjugating the first by \(U\) preserves its degree
278: through \(U_t=\cos(t\chi)+j\sin(t\chi)\); both collapsed ends remain
279: \(\theta\)-independent. The axis \(UiU^{-1}\) has degree zero.
280: 
281: Factor (GF.15) into \(E_i^{-1}\), \(\exp(iw_i\phi\,\operatorname{Ad}_U(i)/2)\),\newline
282: \(\exp(-iw_i\phi\,\operatorname{Ad}_{UA}(i)/2)\), and
283: \(\exp(id_i\phi\,\operatorname{Ad}_{B_i^{-1}}(i)/2)\). Integration of the positive
284: quaternion volume gives degree \(ad\) for an axis of degree \(d\) and
285: angle \(a\phi/2\), for every integer \(a\). The Maurer product boundary
286: forms vanish at the radial ends and at the constant central-sign
287: angle ends; the parameter faces cancel. Therefore its exact local
288: normal-meridian degree, in path--parameter--circle orientation, is
289: 
290: \[
291:                          I_i=-3w_i+d_i(l_i+3).         \tag{GF.16}
292: \]
293: 
294: For the full tangent derivative, use K52's covering group of
295: \(GL^+\) and its normalized third form. The local normal block in
296: (GF.14) has stable degree \(\deg(\mathrm{left})-\deg(\mathrm{right})\). The base rotation
297: has only the \(\phi\) variable. At \(\phi=2\pi\) its Spin endpoint is
298: \((-1)^{w_i}\), while the normal Spin endpoint has the same sign;
299: their product is \(+1\). Thus the full tangent lift is closed even
300: for odd weights. The same exact boundary-form calculation gives
301: 
302: \[
303:                          W_i=-3w_i-d_i(l_i+3).         \tag{GF.17}
304: \]
305: 
306: The ambient/local frame comparison extends through the annulus
307: times \(D^4\) and has zero third pairing with each capped edge trace.
308: The original nonzero unit factors and higher derivatives are
309: carried by the actual homotopies above, so these are degrees of
310: the full source family, with its original physical circle.
311: 
312: ## GF.4. The resulting actual integral relations
313: 
314: Choose generic normal sections at the two ends of each edge and
315: the fixed vertex-link paths of CT.3. Their three coordinate
316: moduli are nonzero, and the interpolation of squared moduli
317: preserves all axis-tube inequalities. The physical rotations,
318: now with the integer weights in (GF.13), preserve these moduli.
319: The vertex actions are \(\theta\)-independent, because the vertex
320: unit maps commute with the diagonal physical circle.
321: 
322: Cap each \(\theta\)-independent endpoint by \(D^2_\theta\) times \(S^1_\phi\),
323: constant in its \(D^2\) variable. The capped edge trace has class
324: \(I_im_i\) in the common exterior; the vertex trace has zero third
325: class. For the graph loops \(\gamma_j=a_j-a_0\) and the relative
326: two-chains \(x_j\) with those boundaries, the actual positive circle
327: trace and the complex sphere orientation give
328: 
329: \[
330:  \Delta x_j=w_jD_j-w_0D_0,\qquad j=1,2.               \tag{GF.18}
331: \]
332: 
333: This follows on the regular-neighbourhood retract: circle--path
334: orientation on a curve with weight \(w_i\) has degree \(-w_i\), and
335: the boundary identity is \(\partial\Delta=-\Delta\partial\). The
336: integral boundary map identifies the relative three-group with
337: the complex curve sphere basis, exactly as in CT.3. No claim
338: that the two vectors in (GF.18) form a primitive sublattice is
339: needed here.
340: 
341: Let \(V\) be the actual ordinary-product variation matrix on this
342: fan and \(\delta\) its actual ambient stable-derivative vector. The
343: two exact prism identities proved in CT.3 and ST.1 now give
344: 
345: \[
346:  V(w_je_j-w_0e_0)=I_je_j-I_0e_0,\qquad
347:  w_j\delta_j-w_0\delta_0=W_j-W_0,\qquad j=1,2.        \tag{GF.19}
348: \]
349: 
350: The matrix \(V\) is symmetric by the integral doubled-exterior
351: intersection proof of K29, since the boundary meridians generate
352: \(H_3\) and the return fixes its boundary. Equations (GF.19) are
353: complete integral identities for the actual constructed product.
354: They leave the transverse variation/derivative entries to be
355: computed by the remaining global or flop comparison. In particular
356: this proof does not assign the missing central return or replace
357: the needed product-reset map by a scalar-family recurrence.
358: 
359: At \(P=I\), \((l_i)=(0,-6,0)\), \((w_i)=(0,1,-1)\),
360: \((d_i)=(-2,-1,-1)\); the formulas give \(I=(-6,0,0)\) and \(W=(6,-6,6)\).
361: Substitution of the completely audited initial matrices of K51/K52
362: verifies both relations, with every sign retained. This equality
363: is also executed in the accompanying exact certificate.
